1
00:00:02,800 --> 00:00:12,072
For a negative or fractional power, the same formula still works, but the coefficients never become zero, so the series never stops.

2
00:00:12,072 --> 00:00:22,372
One plus x to the n equals one plus n x plus n times n minus one over two factorial x squared, and so on forever.

3
00:00:22,372 --> 00:00:30,419
An infinite series is only useful if it converges, and this one does only when the absolute value of x is less than one.

4
00:00:30,419 --> 00:00:33,211
That condition is part of the answer: always state it.

5
00:00:34,434 --> 00:00:35,347
Watch why.

6
00:00:35,347 --> 00:00:38,173
The white curve is one over one minus x.

7
00:00:38,173 --> 00:00:41,259
The yellow curve adds up the series term by term.

8
00:00:41,259 --> 00:00:48,680
Inside the green band, where x is between minus one and one, the partial sums close in on the true curve.

9
00:00:48,680 --> 00:00:52,577
Outside it, they fly away, no matter how many terms you take.

10
00:00:53,800 --> 00:00:56,367
Four expansions worth knowing by sight.

11
00:00:56,367 --> 00:01:05,330
Notice the signs: with minus x inside a negative power, every term is positive; with plus x, the signs alternate.

12
00:01:06,534 --> 00:01:07,589
Two expansions.

13
00:01:07,800 --> 00:01:14,052
For one minus two x to the half, replace x by minus two x everywhere, powers included.

14
00:01:14,267 --> 00:01:24,254
Simplifying: one minus x minus half x squared minus half x cubed, valid when two x is less than one in size: x less than a half.

15
00:01:24,467 --> 00:01:32,157
For four plus x, first make the bracket start with one: take the four out as four to the minus half, which is a half.

16
00:01:32,367 --> 00:01:36,301
Expand with x over four, and multiply by a half.

17
00:01:36,301 --> 00:01:42,057
Valid when x over four is less than one: x less than four in size, not one.

18
00:01:44,067 --> 00:01:49,277
That is the classic mistake: never expand four plus x directly.

19
00:01:49,277 --> 00:01:54,841
Make it one plus something small first, and apply the condition to the something small.

20
00:01:59,634 --> 00:02:08,685
When x is small, x squared is tiny and x cubed tinier still, so the first two or three terms already give an excellent value.

21
00:02:08,685 --> 00:02:18,216
One point zero two to the power one third: one, plus point zero two over three, minus point zero zero zero four over nine.

22
00:02:18,216 --> 00:02:23,138
The next term is about a millionth and cannot touch the fifth decimal place.

23
00:02:23,138 --> 00:02:25,917
One point zero zero six six two.

24
00:02:27,134 --> 00:02:28,867
The cold open, solved.

25
00:02:28,867 --> 00:02:34,708
Pick the nearest perfect cube, one twenty five, and factor it out so the bracket is close to one.

26
00:02:34,934 --> 00:02:38,839
Five, times one plus point zero one six, to the third.

27
00:02:39,067 --> 00:02:45,481
Expand: one, plus point zero one six over three, minus its square over nine.

28
00:02:45,700 --> 00:02:51,821
Five times one point zero zero five three: five point zero two six five.

29
00:02:51,821 --> 00:02:55,910
The true value is five point zero two six five two six.

30
00:02:57,934 --> 00:02:58,437
Two more.

31
00:02:59,167 --> 00:03:05,152
Root one hundred and two: factor out a hundred, expand: ten point zero nine nine five.

32
00:03:05,367 --> 00:03:14,465
One point zero two to the tenth: ten is a whole number, so the series is finite and exact; we just stop when terms become negligible.

33
00:03:14,667 --> 00:03:15,965
One point two one nine.

34
00:03:18,567 --> 00:03:19,923
Exam tip.

35
00:03:19,923 --> 00:03:27,852
To approximate a root, split the number into the nearest perfect power plus a small remainder, and factor out the power.

36
00:03:27,852 --> 00:03:30,405
Root one oh two: use a hundred.

37
00:03:30,405 --> 00:03:34,347
Cube root of one twenty seven: use one twenty five.

38
00:03:38,700 --> 00:03:42,439
Imagine a bank that pays one hundred percent interest a year.

39
00:03:42,439 --> 00:03:47,360
Compound it twice a year and you get one point five squared, two point two five.

40
00:03:47,360 --> 00:03:48,519
Monthly, more.

41
00:03:48,519 --> 00:03:50,173
Daily, more still.

42
00:03:50,173 --> 00:03:54,001
Yet however often you compound, it never runs away.

43
00:03:54,001 --> 00:03:56,019
It approaches a fixed number: e.

44
00:03:58,234 --> 00:04:07,688
Expand one plus one over n to the n with the binomial theorem, and divide each factor of n in the numerators by an n from the denominator.

45
00:04:07,900 --> 00:04:13,331
As n grows, every bracket one minus k over n tends to one.

46
00:04:13,331 --> 00:04:21,473
So e is one plus one over one factorial plus one over two factorial, and so on: the sum of one over r factorial.

47
00:04:21,700 --> 00:04:27,535
Every term is positive, and the first two make two, so e is more than two.

48
00:04:27,535 --> 00:04:38,675
And from three on, r factorial is bigger than two to the r minus one, so the series is less than one plus a geometric series that sums to two: e is less than three.

49
00:04:38,900 --> 00:04:45,698
Watch the terms pile up on a number line: one, then one more, a half, a sixth, a twenty fourth.

50
00:04:45,698 --> 00:04:55,670
Each is much smaller than the last, and after eight terms the total already agrees with e to four decimal places: two point seven one eight three.

51
00:05:01,467 --> 00:05:09,030
The exponential series: e to the x is the sum of x to the r over r factorial, and it works for every x.

52
00:05:09,234 --> 00:05:14,248
Any a to the x becomes an exponential, because a is e to the log a.

53
00:05:14,467 --> 00:05:21,586
The logarithmic series, log of one plus x, alternates, and only works from minus one up to one.

54
00:05:21,800 --> 00:05:30,499
Replace x by minus x and subtract, and you get a much faster series for log of one plus x over one minus x.

55
00:05:33,700 --> 00:05:34,643
Summing a series.

56
00:05:34,934 --> 00:05:42,250
Cancel one n: the general term n over n factorial is one over n minus one factorial.

57
00:05:42,467 --> 00:05:49,346
So the series is one over zero factorial, plus one over one factorial, and so on: exactly e.

58
00:05:51,367 --> 00:05:52,555
Even factorials.

59
00:05:52,767 --> 00:05:58,753
Add the series for e and e to the minus one: odd terms cancel, even ones double.

60
00:05:58,967 --> 00:06:01,645
Halve it: one point five four three one.

61
00:06:03,667 --> 00:06:04,567
Log two.

62
00:06:04,567 --> 00:06:10,361
Putting x equal to one in the first log series crawls; you would need thousands of terms.

63
00:06:10,361 --> 00:06:11,860
Use the fast one instead.

64
00:06:12,067 --> 00:06:17,841
Choose x so that one plus x over one minus x is two: x is a third.

65
00:06:18,067 --> 00:06:18,761
Four terms.

66
00:06:19,500 --> 00:06:22,548
Zero point six nine three one.

67
00:06:22,548 --> 00:06:26,868
The true value is zero point six nine three one four seven.

68
00:06:30,100 --> 00:06:31,313
Pause and think.

69
00:06:31,313 --> 00:06:34,411
One plus three x to the minus half.

70
00:06:34,411 --> 00:06:35,172
When is it valid?

71
00:06:42,067 --> 00:06:49,334
x less than a third in size, because the quantity in the bracket is three x, and that must be less than one.

72
00:06:49,334 --> 00:06:53,592
Absolute x less than one is the trap: it ignores the three.

73
00:06:55,567 --> 00:06:56,415
One more.

74
00:06:56,415 --> 00:06:59,043
How many terms in a plus b to the ten?

75
00:07:05,934 --> 00:07:06,756
Eleven.

76
00:07:06,756 --> 00:07:08,735
r runs from zero to ten.

77
00:07:08,735 --> 00:07:12,551
Ten is the trap: it forgets the r equals zero term.

78
00:07:14,734 --> 00:07:15,803
Quick recap.

79
00:07:15,803 --> 00:07:21,441
The binomial theorem gives n plus one terms with coefficients n C r.

80
00:07:21,441 --> 00:07:24,254
The general term is T r plus one.

81
00:07:24,254 --> 00:07:27,574
The coefficients sum to two to the n.

82
00:07:27,574 --> 00:07:33,082
For any index the series is infinite and needs x less than one in size.

83
00:07:33,082 --> 00:07:37,978
Approximate roots by splitting off the nearest perfect power.

84
00:07:37,978 --> 00:07:40,435
And e is the sum of one over r factorial.

85
00:07:44,634 --> 00:07:46,263
That was the binomial theorem.

86
00:07:46,263 --> 00:07:52,982
Next, complex numbers: polar form, De Moivre's theorem, and the cube roots of unity.

87
00:07:52,982 --> 00:07:53,524
See you there.

